menu "IDFXX log"
    depends on IDFXX_STD_FORMAT

    config IDFXX_LOG_MAX_SINKS
        int "Maximum number of sinks"
        default 4
        range 1 16
        help
            The maximum number of sinks (idfxx::log::sink) that can be added with
            idfxx::log::add_sink at the same time. The count includes sinks added for every line,
            and sinks that are being removed until the calls into them have finished. Adding a
            sink beyond this limit fails with errc::invalid_state.

    config IDFXX_LOG_ALL_LINES
        bool "Let sinks receive every log line"
        default n
        help
            With this option enabled, a sink can receive every ESP-IDF log line: lines logged with
            ESP_LOGx, ESP_LOG_BUFFER_* and esp_log_write, and by the libraries that log through
            them. Add such a sink with idfxx::log::add_sink(idfxx::log::all_lines, sink).

            To receive every line, idfxx_log wraps esp_log and esp_log_va at link time
            (-Wl,--wrap). The app and its other components must not wrap them as well: a second
            definition of __wrap_esp_log or __wrap_esp_log_va makes the link fail.

            Sinks do not receive early, DRAM or binary-mode lines, or lines more verbose than the
            compile-time maximum level. With LOG_MASTER_LEVEL under log v1, they also do not
            receive ESP_LOGx lines more verbose than the master level, because the macros skip
            those lines before logging them.

            While no such sink is added, each log call makes one extra check and uses about 64
            bytes more stack, for the wrapper's own frame. For the stack that a log call uses
            while such a sink is added, see IDFXX_LOG_SINK_MAX_LINE.

            With this option disabled, sinks receive only the lines logged through idfxx::log,
            nothing is wrapped, and idfxx::log::all_lines is not defined.

    config IDFXX_LOG_SINK_MAX_LINE
        int "Maximum length of an ESP-IDF log line passed to a sink (bytes)"
        depends on IDFXX_LOG_ALL_LINES
        default 256
        range 64 1024
        help
            The maximum length of a line logged through ESP-IDF's log that is passed to a sink
            added with idfxx::log::all_lines. Lines logged through idfxx::log are not limited. A
            longer line is shortened to this limit, at a UTF-8 character boundary.

            The limit includes the prefix that the line's caller formatted before the message,
            even though the sink does not receive the prefix. The prefix is log v1's level,
            timestamp and tag, or whatever a caller of esp_log_write formats itself. For a line
            logged in several calls, the limit also includes the line's tag, which is the tag of
            its first call.

            Each join slot (IDFXX_LOG_JOIN_SLOTS) holds a line of this size. For a line logged in
            several calls, each call copies the line so far, up to this many bytes, with
            interrupts disabled. A large limit therefore adds interrupt latency.

            While such a sink is added, a log call uses up to about this limit plus 64 bytes more
            stack than with IDFXX_LOG_ALL_LINES disabled, plus the stack that the sinks themselves
            use. This was measured on esp32s3 in QEMU with ESP-IDF's console output. The line is
            formatted for the sinks after the console has printed it, not while it prints. If
            printing uses less stack (no console, or a lighter vprintf), a log call therefore
            still uses about as much stack as it does with ESP-IDF's console.

            ESP-IDF's own tasks that have small stacks may need larger ones if they log while such
            a sink is added. One example is the system event task (sys_evt), which has 2304 bytes
            by default (CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_EVENT_TASK_STACK_SIZE).

    config IDFXX_LOG_JOIN_SLOTS
        int "Maximum number of tasks with an unfinished line"
        depends on IDFXX_LOG_ALL_LINES
        default 4
        range 1 32
        help
            Some libraries log one line in several calls. For example, the Wi-Fi libraries log a
            prefix, then the message, then a newline. Each task with an unfinished line holds a
            slot until the line's newline arrives. The line is then passed to the sinks with the
            level and tag of its first call.

            When every slot is in use and another task starts a line, that task takes the slot of
            the task that used its slot least recently, and the unfinished line in that slot is
            discarded. The unfinished line of a deleted task is also discarded. Each slot holds a
            line of up to IDFXX_LOG_SINK_MAX_LINE bytes. See that option for the effect on
            interrupt latency.
endmenu
